    “You got skinny,” he said.
    “You got a haircut. Somebody’s paying for it.”
    “Same thing.”
    For one second, the old face. Then it closed over again.
    He’d ordered for me: scrambled eggs, a side of rice, a café con leche I
hadn’t asked for and would have chosen, and toast cut on the diagonal,
the way my mother cuts it and the way Mikey had watched me eat it
every morning for twelve years on the fourth floor. He’d lined up the
silverware beside his own plate with the parallel precision of a man ar‐
ranging surgical instruments. He buttered his toast in squares.
    I said nothing about it. I picked up my fork.
    “So,” said Mikey.
    “So.”
    “Your face was on the news.” He meant the board hearing, which
had been on the local channel for forty seconds, between a fire in Mott
Haven and a segment about a raccoon. “You looked good. You looked
like a guy who’d been told to be boring.”
    “I was told to be boring.”
    “You can’t.” He almost smiled. “Never could.”
    We ate. It was easier than I’d expected. For ten minutes it was almost
a normal breakfast, two guys in a booth, talking about the Soundview
kid’s brother, who’d once tried to carry a moped up four flights be‐
cause he didn’t trust the lock. Mikey said it was a bicycle. I said it was a
moped. He said my memory was broken. I said I had evidence of that.
He laughed, once, short, startled, like a sound he’d forgotten he owned.
    Then he put down his fork.
    “I have to say a thing,” he said. “And I need you to let me say it be‐
fore you say yours.”
    I nodded.
    “Vale wants you at the Armory. Bring the installation, do the
